Moved onto the board from the roadmap block in CLAUDE.md, which was removed
because it had never been reconciled with the board it pointed at. This is one
of the two items in it that are real parity gaps.

kanban-md has this: cmd/metrics.go, "Show flow metrics -- throughput, average
lead/cycle time, flow efficiency, and aging work items". The metric names in
its code are throughput, lead_time, cycle_time, flow_efficiency and wip.

karr has the data already. The started / completed timestamps are stamped on
every status change and have been correct on non-default boards since the
terminal-status sweep (#67, #98, #101), and per-command activity logging (#64)
records refs/karr/log/* entries that carry who did what and when. Two comments
in Task.pm already name the cycle-time arithmetic as this command's job.

What needs deciding rather than assuming, and best answered by reading
kanban-md's implementation rather than reinventing it:

- Which timestamps define lead versus cycle time here -- created to completed,
  versus started to completed, is the usual split, but kanban-md's choice is
  the one that keeps the numbers comparable between the two tools.
- What flow efficiency divides by, given karr records blocked state
  (blocked / block_reason) but not blocked durations.
- Whether the window is fixed (last N days) or derived, and what an empty
  board answers.
- Whether it reads task timestamps only, or also the activity log -- the log
  is richer but only exists for boards that were active after #64 landed, so
  a metrics command built on it would report nothing for older boards without
  saying why.

--json belongs on it from the start, since a metrics command exists to be
consumed.
